{"exhaustive":{"nbHits":false,"typo":false},"exhaustiveNbHits":false,"exhaustiveTypo":false,"hits":[{"_highlightResult":{"author":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"richardmeng"},"story_text":{"fullyHighlighted":false,"matchLevel":"full","matchedWords":["access","denied","agent"],"value":"Hi HN! We built nonce, an open-source tool that sits between personal AI <em>agents</em> (Muse, Grok, ChatGPT, Claude) and your personal data in Gmail, Calendar, Drive and more.<p>Today, if you connect Gmail to an AI <em>agent</em> like Muse, it can read your entire inbox. Whether on purpose or not, they can read far more emails than needed. This poses a huge data-privilege risk, in which personal data is accessed without limits.<p>With nonce, the AI will no longer hold your Google or Slack tokens. To read anything, it has to show its intent first:<p>User's question: When does my flight to Tokyo leave?\n<em>Agent</em>'s justification: Need the airline confirmation email\n<em>Agent</em>'s search query: from:united.com Tokyo\n<em>Access</em> needed: 10 minutes<p>If the justification and search query are reasonable, nonce issues a scoped token to the AI <em>Agent</em>. AI <em>Agent</em> can only query data using a token.<p>If something looks fishy, i.e the AI is acting on its own, wants too much, or wants sensitive emails like bank statements, medical results, or password resets. nonce sends you a notification to approve or deny.<p>By default, nonce <em>denies</em> massive crawling behaviors<p>How it works\n1. nonce scores each search against your original question before running it, to ensure its relevance.\n2. Every search is combined with the token's search. If the AI searches &quot;bank statement&quot;, nonce runs (from:united.com Tokyo) AND (bank statement). It can narrow its <em>access</em>, but not widen it.\n3. The token is scoped to that <em>agent</em> only, with a TTL for X minutes.<p>Limitations: nonce can't see your chat with the AI, so it can't prove you asked, which leaves a risk that the AI <em>Agent</em> can fake the intent. However, users can audit past accesses in nonce portal and identify these bypasses.<p>Give it a shot at <a href=\"https://github.com/roe-ai/nonce\" rel=\"nofollow\">https://github.com/roe-ai/nonce</a> We'd love to hear what you think.<p>Website: <a href=\"https://anonce.io\" rel=\"nofollow\">https://anonce.io</a>"},"title":{"fullyHighlighted":false,"matchLevel":"partial","matchedWords":["access","agent"],"value":"Show HN: Nonce \u2013 Open-source, least-privilege Gmail <em>access</em> for AI <em>agents</em>"},"url":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"https://www.anonce.io/"}},"_tags":["story","author_richardmeng","story_50330213","show_hn"],"author":"richardmeng","children":[50332171],"created_at":"2026-11-01T21:44:21Z","created_at_i":1793569461,"num_comments":0,"objectID":"50330213","points":1,"story_id":50330213,"story_text":"Hi HN! We built nonce, an open-source tool that sits between personal AI agents (Muse, Grok, ChatGPT, Claude) and your personal data in Gmail, Calendar, Drive and more.<p>Today, if you connect Gmail to an AI agent like Muse, it can read your entire inbox. Whether on purpose or not, they can read far more emails than needed. This poses a huge data-privilege risk, in which personal data is accessed without limits.<p>With nonce, the AI will no longer hold your Google or Slack tokens. To read anything, it has to show its intent first:<p>User&#x27;s question: When does my flight to Tokyo leave?\nAgent&#x27;s justification: Need the airline confirmation email\nAgent&#x27;s search query: from:united.com Tokyo\nAccess needed: 10 minutes<p>If the justification and search query are reasonable, nonce issues a scoped token to the AI Agent. AI Agent can only query data using a token.<p>If something looks fishy, i.e the AI is acting on its own, wants too much, or wants sensitive emails like bank statements, medical results, or password resets. nonce sends you a notification to approve or deny.<p>By default, nonce denies massive crawling behaviors<p>How it works\n1. nonce scores each search against your original question before running it, to ensure its relevance.\n2. Every search is combined with the token&#x27;s search. If the AI searches &quot;bank statement&quot;, nonce runs (from:united.com Tokyo) AND (bank statement). It can narrow its access, but not widen it.\n3. The token is scoped to that agent only, with a TTL for X minutes.<p>Limitations: nonce can&#x27;t see your chat with the AI, so it can&#x27;t prove you asked, which leaves a risk that the AI Agent can fake the intent. However, users can audit past accesses in nonce portal and identify these bypasses.<p>Give it a shot at <a href=\"https:&#x2F;&#x2F;github.com&#x2F;roe-ai&#x2F;nonce\" rel=\"nofollow\">https:&#x2F;&#x2F;github.com&#x2F;roe-ai&#x2F;nonce</a> We&#x27;d love to hear what you think.<p>Website: <a href=\"https:&#x2F;&#x2F;anonce.io\" rel=\"nofollow\">https:&#x2F;&#x2F;anonce.io</a>","title":"Show HN: Nonce \u2013 Open-source, least-privilege Gmail access for AI agents","updated_at":"2026-11-02T02:02:12Z","url":"https://www.anonce.io/"},{"_highlightResult":{"author":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"shivsarthak"},"comment_text":{"fullyHighlighted":false,"matchLevel":"full","matchedWords":["access","denied","agent"],"value":"I built Doozy because support at my company (Spur) is understaffed and kept waiting on engineering to run a query for a customer, or to check whether something was a bug. The first version was a Slack bot with read <em>access</em> to our Postgres and Stripe, and it was very hacky. It could barely read files sent in a thread, but the <em>agent</em> was smart enough to find them through Slack search and finish the task anyway. It got used far more by support and success than by engineers, which surprised me. 13 people at Spur use it every day; it's basically a critical part of the team now.<p>Doozy is the rebuilt, multi-tenant version. You mention it in a thread with a task, it runs the task against your systems (database, Stripe, GitHub, Linear, PostHog, any MCP server, the web) in its own isolated environment, and posts the result and files in the thread.<p>How it touches production:<p>- Every job runs in a fresh sandbox (E2B today) with no credentials in it. Model calls, tool calls and git go through gateways outside the sandbox that add credentials per request. A prompt injection can make the <em>agent</em> misuse the tools it has, but it can't exfiltrate a key, because there isn't one to read.\n- Secrets the <em>agent</em> genuinely needs inside the sandbox (an SSH key, a CLI token) are handed over only when it asks for a named secret with a reason, and that request is logged.\n- Tools marked destructive start <em>denied</em>. Owners turn them on per tool.\n- Every external action writes a receipt in the thread and an append-only audit log.<p>Per-workspace pricing, no seats. 14-day trial, no card, install takes a couple of minutes.<p>Happy to go deep on the sandbox and gateway design."},"story_title":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"Show HN: Doozy \u2013 an AI coworker in Slack that works in prod without your keys"},"story_url":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"https://askdoozy.com/"}},"_tags":["comment","author_shivsarthak","story_50166515"],"author":"shivsarthak","children":[50166959],"comment_text":"I built Doozy because support at my company (Spur) is understaffed and kept waiting on engineering to run a query for a customer, or to check whether something was a bug. The first version was a Slack bot with read access to our Postgres and Stripe, and it was very hacky. It could barely read files sent in a thread, but the agent was smart enough to find them through Slack search and finish the task anyway. It got used far more by support and success than by engineers, which surprised me. 13 people at Spur use it every day; it&#x27;s basically a critical part of the team now.<p>Doozy is the rebuilt, multi-tenant version. You mention it in a thread with a task, it runs the task against your systems (database, Stripe, GitHub, Linear, PostHog, any MCP server, the web) in its own isolated environment, and posts the result and files in the thread.<p>How it touches production:<p>- Every job runs in a fresh sandbox (E2B today) with no credentials in it. Model calls, tool calls and git go through gateways outside the sandbox that add credentials per request. A prompt injection can make the agent misuse the tools it has, but it can&#x27;t exfiltrate a key, because there isn&#x27;t one to read.\n- Secrets the agent genuinely needs inside the sandbox (an SSH key, a CLI token) are handed over only when it asks for a named secret with a reason, and that request is logged.\n- Tools marked destructive start denied. Owners turn them on per tool.\n- Every external action writes a receipt in the thread and an append-only audit log.<p>Per-workspace pricing, no seats. 14-day trial, no card, install takes a couple of minutes.<p>Happy to go deep on the sandbox and gateway design.","created_at":"2026-10-20T13:59:49Z","created_at_i":1792504789,"objectID":"50166597","parent_id":50166515,"story_id":50166515,"story_title":"Show HN: Doozy \u2013 an AI coworker in Slack that works in prod without your keys","story_url":"https://askdoozy.com/","updated_at":"2026-10-20T23:19:20Z"}],"hitsPerPage":2,"nbHits":3,"nbPages":2,"page":0,"params":"query=access+denied+agent&tags=%28story%2Ccomment%29&hitsPerPage=2&page=0&numericFilters=created_at_i%3E%3D1791004054%2Ccreated_at_i%3C%3D1793596054&advancedSyntax=true&analyticsTags=backend","processingTimeMS":7,"processingTimingsMS":{"_request":{"queue":20,"roundTrip":15},"fetch":{"query":6,"total":7},"total":7},"query":"access denied agent","serverTimeMS":28}
